WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.317
Zoe Parham: There is a message that has

00:00:01.317 --> 00:00:02.891
gone out to everyone abroad,

00:00:02.891 --> 00:00:05.257
I think it's from the state
department, that says,

00:00:05.257 --> 00:00:07.890
"Come home now, or you might
not be able to come home."

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.263
That message came out after we were stuck.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.848
After we were stuck in Peru.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.440
I bought a United flight

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.300
that was going to go from
Lima to Houston to Denver.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.825
I had my boyfriend call United

00:00:24.825 --> 00:00:26.300
just to confirm everything,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.783
and my flight had been canceled,

00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:30.224
and I was never notified.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.660
And that's when I started
to feel like, that's weird,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.670
you know, the quarantine
is supposed to be over.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.280
Like, why don't I have a flight anymore?

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.490
Arturo Salgado: When we came,
India only had three cases,

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:47.270
and right now they have over 500.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.030
Literally, from one day to the next,

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.800
everything just changed.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.707
When we got to Mumbai, it was like:

00:00:56.707 --> 00:00:58.787
"OK, we're closing everything down.

00:00:58.787 --> 00:01:00.207
There are no more flights."

00:01:00.207 --> 00:01:01.273
Brian Johnson: I've already been rebooked

00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:03.456
eight to 10 times since last Tuesday.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.753
If I didn't have a wife and
kids to get back home to,

00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:13.098
I think I would've just stayed
in Austria and just given up.

00:01:13.098 --> 00:01:15.910
Parham: United won't be
flying back until May.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.030
I've heard the same thing about American.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.670
Things are not just returning to normal

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:20.750
when the quarantine is over.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.260
And there've been a lot of rumors

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.320
that the quarantine is
just going to be extended.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.447
Johnson: In Austria they said,

00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:26.657
"OK, what we're gonna do

00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:28.720
is shut down nonessential shops."

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.898
And I'm thinking that's
not too big of a deal.

00:01:30.898 --> 00:01:33.725
The next day or two, all
the restaurants shut down.

00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:35.154
I have no kitchenette.

00:01:35.154 --> 00:01:37.400
I couldn't even find a
supermarket that was open.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.110
I didn't really know what I was gonna do.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.876
Salgado: The curfew here in India has been

00:01:40.876 --> 00:01:44.134
extended until April 14.

00:01:44.134 --> 00:01:47.520
That means there are no buses, no flights.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.660
No means of transportation anywhere.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.168
Elizabeth Lord: These
restrictions have been put

00:01:51.168 --> 00:01:53.108
in rapid fire without a lot of recourse

00:01:53.108 --> 00:01:55.100
for people that are on the road.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.431
Some people were on camping trips

00:02:00.431 --> 00:02:01.990
or in northern districts,

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.140
which takes either a domestic flight,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.460
which are also suspended,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.500
or many, many hours of
travel by the roads.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.040
Salgado: They just made
an announcement today

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.900
that all foreigners that
arrived after February 15

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.370
have to stay inside.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:20.020
So that's making it a little
difficult for us to buy food.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.067
Parham: A lot of people
have made comments like,

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:25.230
"Wow, what a nice place to be stuck."

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.760
And I disagree.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.270
This quarantine is
enforced by martial law.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.928
You can only leave your home

00:02:30.928 --> 00:02:33.340
between 5 a.m. and 8 p.m.,

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.440
and it's only to go to a bank,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.586
pharmacy, or grocery store. That's it.

00:02:37.586 --> 00:02:40.120
We step outside to go
to the grocery store,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.450
and we have to talk to police

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.650
and tell them where we're going.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.690
Johnson: I stay in this small
little hotel when I'm there,

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.980
'cause it's a great location,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.380
right in the center of Vienna.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.040
The weird thing is that the streets

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.340
and everything are so empty. So empty.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.173
They don't play around.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:54.710
But if they catch you
doing something obvious,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.060
like shaking hands or
hugging, you will get fined.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.820
And I heard it was, like,
as much as 600 euros.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.020
Salgado: There are a lot of hotels here

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.792
and hostels that are not
taking any foreigners.

00:03:04.792 --> 00:03:06.590
Lord: There was an
article in the India Times

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.350
about a guesthouse owner
being booked and arrested

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.960
for having accepted foreigners.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.266
That story alone can create a lot of fear

00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:17.120
for people in a position to take us in.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.870
Salgado: We were very lucky to find

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.720
a hostel here in Bandra in Mumbai.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.950
Lord: So, right now we're
obeying the lockdown.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:24.950
We haven't left our hotel,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.355
which is a nice hotel that we called ahead

00:03:27.355 --> 00:03:28.914
and said that they would accept us.

00:03:28.914 --> 00:03:31.060
But we're gonna be
running out of funds soon.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.760
They're not providing any meals.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.410
We don't have any choice but to pay

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.310
the hotel restaurant for all of our food.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.580
Grocery shopping is, in theory, available.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.880
But with the stories that I've heard

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.492
about foreigners, you
know, being threatened

00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:45.530
with violence, we don't feel as though

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.587
we can leave just to get basic supplies.

00:03:48.587 --> 00:03:50.730
Salgado: We were trying
to buy food yesterday,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.270
and people did not want
to sell us anything

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:55.670
because we were foreigners.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.707
They simply said: "Go away.

00:03:57.707 --> 00:03:59.277
We are closed for the night.

00:03:59.277 --> 00:04:00.520
We're closed for the day."

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.760
It's been a little difficult.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.670
We came across discrimination, even.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.590
Just two days ago we were walking

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.430
in the street, looking for food,

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.263
and people started yelling

00:04:09.263 --> 00:04:11.438
"corona" at me and my friend.

00:04:11.438 --> 00:04:13.230
Johnson: So, at the point I said, OK,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.700
it's time to reach out to the embassy.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.397
The reply, and I kid you not,
was one sentence that said,

00:04:18.397 --> 00:04:21.059
"If you're having problems
finding a flight back,

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:23.591
well, we recommend that you try Expedia

00:04:23.591 --> 00:04:25.370
or Google Flights to find an alternate."

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.380
That was the US Embassy's reply to me.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.924
Lord: Our first call to the US Embassy

00:04:29.924 --> 00:04:31.470
was on Saturday the 21st.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.160
They highly recommended that
we get out before the 22nd,

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.720
which at that point was 12 hours away,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.390
which was when the suspension

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.305
of the international
flights was happening.

00:04:40.305 --> 00:04:43.120
And we had just been
able to secure our entry

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.656
into Delhi and to get to a hotel,

00:04:44.656 --> 00:04:46.173
so the traveling that we were

00:04:46.173 --> 00:04:48.214
doing on land was difficult,

00:04:48.214 --> 00:04:49.710
and we weren't able to make that happen.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.870
Parham: The US Embassy hasn't really

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.840
gotten back to our situation directly.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.590
I've emailed them so many times,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.515
and it says to only email them once

00:04:57.515 --> 00:04:58.730
and wait for a response,

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:00.413
but when I finally do get responses,

00:05:00.413 --> 00:05:02.271
they're just automated.

00:05:02.271 --> 00:05:04.090
I do not want to be forgotten here.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:05.370
Lord: It's very difficult for someone

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.290
that has never had dealings
with the US Embassy

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.070
not to know what you can
ask, like repatriation loans.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.678
I didn't know that that was a thing

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:14.261
until, you know, two days later.

00:05:14.261 --> 00:05:15.950
Salgado: We're thinking,
is it gonna be a week,

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.570
two weeks, a month, two months?

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.150
That's what's stressing us out right now.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.360
Lord: We might be here
for the full 21 days,

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.000
and then maybe be hoping
that it's only 21 days.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.633
And then, you know, I would have to do

00:05:26.633 --> 00:05:28.368
what the embassy suggested, which is start

00:05:28.368 --> 00:05:30.780
sourcing funds from
friends and family at home.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.584
Parham: I'm hoping that the US Embassy

00:05:32.584 --> 00:05:35.110
can organize a car for us

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.775
with the proper documentation

00:05:36.775 --> 00:05:38.900
and at least get us to Lima.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.252
So we're available for when our names

00:05:41.252 --> 00:05:42.990
do come up on the chartered flights.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.340
Salgado: We're willing to do anything

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.590
to get back home at this point.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.124
Whether it's paying for a flight,

00:05:47.124 --> 00:05:49.991
travel for two days if
necessary. Anything.

00:05:49.991 --> 00:05:51.900
We just want to get back
home to our families.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.300
Parham: In a time that's filled
with so much uncertainty,

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.350
you just want to be close to your family.

